POS DAY RECONCILIATION

Pos Day Reconciliation — a real-world task using this week's concepts: creating and building lists, append(), looping over a list, sum(), min(), max(), sorted(), len(), counting with variables, while validation, comparisons, and 2-decimal money format. No imports, no tricks.

Solve it individually, then come together as a group to compare approaches. Good luck!

A POS agent at a junction closes business at 7pm and must reconcile the day's transactions before returning the cash to the shop owner. One program, the full picture.

The program must:
- Ask "How many sales today: " and keep asking until it is at least 1.
- Ask each sale amount in order (Sale 1: , Sale 2: ... - decimals allowed) and build them into a list. While collecting, remember WHICH sale number was the biggest - if two sales tie, the FIRST one keeps the crown (same rule as your Day 8 ajo program).
- Then print exactly six lines:
  SALES TODAY: 6
  TOTAL: N41250.00
  AVERAGE: N6875.00
  BIGGEST: N15000.00 (sale number 3)
  ABOVE N5000: 3
  BEST TO WORST: N15000.00, N9000.00, ... (all sales, biggest first, two decimals, joined with commas)

Sample run:

How many sales today: 3
Sale 1: 2500
Sale 2: 15000
Sale 3: 9000

SALES TODAY: 3
TOTAL: N26500.00
AVERAGE: N8833.33
BIGGEST: N15000.00 (sale number 2)
ABOVE N5000: 2
BEST TO WORST: N15000.00, N9000.00, N2500.00

Edge cases to handle: a single sale (it is total, average, biggest and worst all at once), two sales tied for biggest (the earlier sale number wins), every sale below 5000 (ABOVE N5000: 0), decimal sales like 750.50 flowing through every line, and checking BEST TO WORST is truly biggest to smallest.
